format 1
adapter safety 1
section: happens

card safety-get-inside-stay-inside-stay-tuned
title: Get inside, stay inside, stay tuned
icon: Home2
references: ref-ready-radiation
summary: The official first steps in a radiation emergency.
steps: Get inside: if warned of a radiation hazard, go straight into the nearest building and move away from windows.
steps: Stay inside: sheltering usually lasts at least 24 hours.
steps: Stay tuned: follow officials' instructions. A battery or hand-crank radio works if other communication fails.
steps: Stay in the most protective spot for the first 24 hours unless a hazard threatens or officials say it is safe.

card safety-good-shelter
title: What makes a good shelter
icon: Buildings2
entry: shielding
references: ref-ready-radiation
summary: More walls between you and the outdoors means more protection.
steps: Put as many walls as you can between yourself and the outside, and move away from windows.
steps: The safest buildings have brick or concrete walls.
steps: Underground parking garages and subways can also provide good shelter.
steps: The best spot is a basement or the center of a large building.
steps: Close windows and doors, and turn off fans, air conditioners and forced-air heating that bring in outside air.

card safety-dont-go-out-to-look
title: Why not go out to look
icon: Warning2
references: ref-ready-radiation
summary: Staying put protects you more than going out to see or to travel.
steps: Radiation levels decrease rapidly during the first 24 hours, becoming significantly less dangerous.
steps: If officials advise you to evacuate, listen for information about routes, shelters and procedures.
steps: If you have evacuated, do not return until local authorities say it is safe.
steps: Leave early only for an immediate hazard such as a fire, gas leak or building collapse, or when officials say so.

card safety-if-you-were-outside
title: If you were outside
icon: Drop
references: ref-ready-radiation
summary: Officials say to get inside, take off outer clothing and wash.
steps: Remove your outer layer of clothing. It can remove up to 90% of radioactive material.
steps: Take clothes off carefully, so you do not shake loose any radioactive dust. Avoid touching your eyes, nose and mouth.
steps: Take a shower or wash with soap and water to remove radioactive particles from skin and hair.
steps: Do not use household cleaning wipes on skin. Hand sanitizer does not protect against radioactive material.
steps: Unsealed foods that were outside may be contaminated with radioactive material.

card safety-potassium-iodide
title: Potassium iodide (KI)
icon: Health
references: ref-cdc-potassium-iodide
summary: Take it only if officials say so. It protects the thyroid only.
steps: Do not take KI unless public health or emergency officials or a healthcare provider tell you to.
steps: KI protects only the thyroid. It does not protect other parts of the body.
steps: It protects only against radioactive iodine, not other types of radioactive materials.
steps: Table salt or iodine-rich foods are not a substitute for KI.
